101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (The WebSocket protocol \(RFC 6455, 2011\) was the main thing I used for real-time communication in this project. WebSockets allow continuous two-way communication between clients, which makes them ideal for systems where low latency is important. This is used directly in Can I Hinder?, where spectator actions need to be sent to the game and applied instantly.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (Coulouris et al. \(2011\) helped shape how I structured the system. Their work on distributed systems explains how a system can be split into separate parts that communicate over a network. This is reflected in the project, which is made up of a C++ game client, a Node.js relay server, a REST API, and a PWA. Splitting it this way made it easier to manage each part while still keeping everything connected in real time.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (Enemy movement is based on steering behaviours introduced by Reynolds \(1999\) and later expanded by Millington and Funge \(2009\). These behaviours, such as Seek, Arrive, Pursue, Wander, and Flee, are a simple way of creating more natural enemy movement. I implemented these in a separate AIBehaviour class, along with wall avoidance and separation, so they could be adjusted without affecting other parts of the game.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (The React documentation \(React Team, 2024\) was used when building the PWA. It helped with structuring components and managing state, which was important for making sure the interface was responsive and easy to use. Using a PWA also meant spectators could join quickly without needing to install anything.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
