109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (The project was developed over 3 main milestones, with each phase building upon the previous one and gradually turning the system into a complete and playable experience.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (The first milestone, which ran from September to Christmas, focused on building the core technical foundation of the project. This included setting up rendering, player movement, collison and the initial WebSocket commuinication between the game and the PWA . By the end of this phase, the game could load maps move an animated player and respond to basic spectator input, which established the core interaction that the rest of the project builds on.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (The second milestone focused on expanding both the gameplay and the networking side of the system, deploying the backend and frontend services to Azure, and developing the main game play loop. The PWA was also improved to provide better user experience, making it easier for spectators to interact with the game. This phase was important in turning the project from a technical prototype into something that felt more like a complete game.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (The final milestone focused on completing and refining the overall experience. This included adding visual effects, balancing the help and hinder mechanics, better UI for the PWA within the radial menu, adding a new unlockable help and hinder button after the desired amount is reached, and a turn based battle system was also introduced for the final level, changing the pace of the gameplay to a more structured encounter compared to the real-time exploration. This particular feature was implemented as it pays homage to why I wanted to pursue game development.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 69 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [306 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 69 0 R
/T (Major Technical Achievements)
/E (Major Technical Achievements)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (One of the main technical achievements of this project is the successful implementation of real-time communication between multiple systems. The game client, WebSocket relay server, REST API, and PWA all communicate together, allowing spectator actions to be sent and applied in the game almost instantly. Getting this working reliably without affecting gameplay performance was a key part of the project.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (Another major achievement is the overall system architecture. The project is split into separate components, including the C++ SFML game, Node.js relay server, ASP.NET Core API, and React-based PWA. Designing the system in this way made it easier to develop and test each part independently, while still allowing them to work together as a single system.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (The implementation of the help and hinder system is also a significant achievement. This system allows spectators to directly influence gameplay in real time, with actions that can either assist or disrupt the player. The addition of progression-based unlocks, where stronger actions become availiable after a certain number of inputs, helped create a more engaging and structured interaction system.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Another key technical achievement is the AI behaviour system used for enemies. Steering behaviours such as Seek, Arrive, Pursue, Wander, and Flee were implemented, along with additional features such as separation and wall avoidance. These systems allowed enemies to move in a more natural and responsive way, improving the overall gameplay experience.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (The final level turn-based battle system is also an important addition. This introduced a different style of gameplay compared to the rest of the project, requiring a separate structure for handling player and enemy actions. Implementing both real-time and turn-based systems within the same project required careful design to ensure they worked together without conflict.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Finally, deploying the backend services and PWA to Azure was a key achievement. This allowed the system to move beyond a local setup and function as a fully connected, real-world application, making it possible for spectators to interact with the game remotely.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
